WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.515
I awoke and found myself still tossing in the sea.

2
00:00:04.516 --> 00:00:12.185
The sun was rising behind Spyglass Hill, and from my perspective, I could see one side of the hill descending into the sea.

3
00:00:12.186 --> 00:00:20.825
This side of Spyglass was bare, with cliffs about fifty feet high and fallen rock spilling into the ocean at its base.

4
00:00:20.826 --> 00:00:27.890
I was about a quarter of a mile out at sea and my first thought was to paddle in, but I soon gave up that idea.

5
00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:32.486
"Just look at those huge waves crashing on the rocks," I said to myself.

6
00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:39.988
"I’ll be dashed to pieces. Even if I could make it through the waves, I’d die trying to climb the cliffs."

7
00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:47.771
In addition to the waves and the rocks, there were strange monsters crawling together in large groups on the bigger, flatter rocks. They

8
00:00:47.772 --> 00:00:57.083
looked like giant soft snails, and when they roared, the rocky cliffs echoed. Some dove into the sea with loud splashes. I’ve

9
00:00:57.084 --> 00:01:03.593
since discovered they were harmless sea lions, but they were another reason I didn’t want to land on that shore. "I’d

10
00:01:03.594 --> 00:01:07.764
rather starve at sea," I thought. I

11
00:01:07.765 --> 00:01:13.477
remembered that the map indicated that the Cape of the Woods was south of my position. The

12
00:01:13.478 --> 00:01:19.334
cape had a long stretch of yellow sand, and I felt I’d have a better chance landing there. The

13
00:01:19.335 --> 00:01:26.987
smooth, blue swells of the sea rose and fell, and lucky for me, my little boat rode them well. I

14
00:01:26.988 --> 00:01:33.129
lay still at the bottom of the boat and peered up at the big, blue waves rising close by. "Thank

15
00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.104
God for Ben Gunn’s little boat," I thought. I

16
00:01:37.105 --> 00:01:40.297
sat up and tried to paddle a little. Immediately,

17
00:01:40.298 --> 00:01:48.624
the movement made the boat unsteady, and it ran straight down a steep slope of water, striking the next wave nose first. Drenched

18
00:01:48.625 --> 00:01:53.419
and terrified, I quickly lay back down again. "I’ll

19
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.546
never reach land!" I cried, frightened. But

20
00:01:57.547 --> 00:02:01.782
the boat instantly righted itself among the surging waves. "I

21
00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:10.501
mustn’t panic," I told myself. "If I don’t disturb the boat’s balance, I can paddle over one side in between swells." It was

22
00:02:10.502 --> 00:02:15.169
slow and tiring work, but I made progress. Even though

23
00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:22.180
I was still too far out from Cape of the Woods to land, I knew I’d surely hit the beach at some point. But I

24
00:02:22.181 --> 00:02:31.220
was tortured by thirst. The harsh sun on my body and the sea salt on my lips made my throat burn and my brain ache. The trees

25
00:02:31.221 --> 00:02:36.489
on the beach looked so close, and I felt sick with longing. Then suddenly

26
00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.239
I saw a familiar sight. Not half

27
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.316
a mile away, the Hispaniola was sailing north. "I don’t

28
00:02:45.317 --> 00:02:49.700
care if they capture me," I thought. "I need water." The Hispaniola

29
00:02:49.701 --> 00:02:55.382
’s beautiful, white canvas sails shone like silver in the sun, and

30
00:02:55.383 --> 00:03:03.280
I guessed that the men on board were sailing around the island and back to the anchorage. But soon, the ship turned westward. "They

31
00:03:03.281 --> 00:03:08.070
see me," I thought. "They’re turning around to pick me up." But

32
00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:13.827
then the Hispaniola ’s sails suddenly lost the wind, and the ship stood there, helpless.

33
00:03:13.828 --> 00:03:17.726
"Stupid fellows," I said. "They’re still drunk."

34
00:03:17.727 --> 00:03:27.514
A breeze came up and the sails gradually filled again; the Hispaniola sailed swiftly for a minute, and then once more lost the wind.

35
00:03:27.515 --> 00:03:37.539
This happened again and again, to and fro, up and down, north, south, east, and west, as the Hispaniola ’s sails flapped

36
00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:43.722
idly. "Nobody’s steering!" I realized. "If I can board the ship, I can return it to the captain.

37
00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:48.491
And there’s drinking water on board! I can taste it already."

38
00:03:48.492 --> 00:03:55.272
The thought of a cool drink motivated me to try to reach the ship, so I sat up and started paddling.

39
00:03:55.273 --> 00:03:59.021
I had to stop and bail water out of the boat occasionally,

40
00:03:59.022 --> 00:04:06.378
but I swiftly learned to glide through the waves, with only occasional blasts of water and foam hitting me in the face.

41
00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:14.747
I quickly neared the Hispaniola and could see that even though the ship was jerking back and forth, no one appeared upon her decks.

42
00:04:14.748 --> 00:04:20.514
"She must be deserted," I thought. "If not, the men are lying drunk below.

43
00:04:20.515 --> 00:04:23.571
When I get on board, I’ll lock them in the cabin."

44
00:04:23.572 --> 00:04:29.169
At last, the wind died down, and the Hispaniola revolved slowly in the current.

45
00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:34.548
The stern cabin window was still open, and the lamp over the table was still burning.

46
00:04:34.549 --> 00:04:38.388
The mainsail drooped like a limp rag.

47
00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:44.460
"Finally the ship has stopped," I said to myself, relieved. "Now I can board her."

48
00:04:44.461 --> 00:04:52.855
But one second later, the wind started up again, and the next thing I knew, the Hispaniola was sailing straight toward me!

49
00:04:52.856 --> 00:04:57.113
"I’ll be crushed!" I cried out. "Help me!"

50
00:04:57.114 --> 00:05:04.379
No one answered; no one was there, so I closed my eyes and waited for death.

51
00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:11.212
Miraculously, at the last moment, the ship suddenly turned a few feet to the right from its lethal course; this

52
00:05:11.213 --> 00:05:15.418
movement brought the huge vessel right up next to me. The

53
00:05:15.419 --> 00:05:20.744
Hispaniola looked immensely tall from my low position in Ben Gunn’s boat. But

54
00:05:20.745 --> 00:05:28.549
within moments, a huge swell rose up underneath me, and the ship’s bowsprit was right over my head. Without

55
00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:38.663
thinking, I leaped. I grabbed onto the long pole sticking out from the front of the ship with one hand and wedged my foot between two ropes. As

56
00:05:38.664 --> 00:05:46.531
I clung there, panting, a dull and sickening thud told me that the ship had struck and crushed Ben Gunn’s boat. I

57
00:05:46.532 --> 00:05:55.966
was saved, but now I had no way to get off the Hispaniola .

